Title: The Velveteen Rabbit
Credit: Written by
Author: Margery Williams
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 8/1/2026
Notes: Medium = stylized children's picture-book illustration; painted storybook look, soft watercolor and ink, warm nursery palette.

FADE IN:

EXT. HOUSE - CHRISTMAS MORNING

Snow dusts a quiet cottage roof. A nursery window glows warm gold.

(SOUND: distant church bells, soft wind in bare branches)

INT. NURSERY - CHRISTMAS MORNING

A child's stocking bulges on the bedpost. Wedged at the top sits the VELVETEEN RABBIT — fat and bunchy, spotted brown and white velveteen, real thread whiskers, ears lined with pink sateen, a sprig of holly between his paws.

(SOUND: tissue paper rustling, children's excited murmurs below)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
There was once a velveteen rabbit, and in the beginning he was really splendid.

Nuts, oranges, a toy engine, and a clockwork mouse tumble from the stocking. THE BOY — about seven, tousled fair hair, nightshirt — hugs the Rabbit tight for a long moment, eyes shining.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
For at least two hours the Boy loved him.

Aunts and Uncles flood in with parcels. Tissue paper flies. In the rush of new presents, the Velveteen Rabbit is set aside on the nursery floor, forgotten.

(SOUND: laughter, paper tearing, footsteps fading)

INT. NURSERY - DAY

The toy cupboard stands open. Expensive mechanical toys gleam on the shelf. The MODEL BOAT, paint worn, sits stiffly. TIMOTHY, a jointed wooden lion, holds his head high. The Velveteen Rabbit lies alone on the floor near the fender, shy and still.

(SOUND: clock ticking, faint nursery hush)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
For a long time he lived in the toy cupboard or on the nursery floor, and no one thought very much about him.

The mechanical toys look down on him. The Model Boat turns a superior painted eye.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
They were full of modern ideas, and pretended they were real.

Beside the Rabbit lies the SKIN HORSE — very old, brown coat bald in patches showing seams, tail hairs sparse and pulled. Kind eyes. He alone stays near the Rabbit.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The only person who was kind to him at all was the Skin Horse.

INT. NURSERY - NIGHT

Moonlight falls across the fender. The Velveteen Rabbit and the Skin Horse lie side by side. The house is quiet.

(SOUND: soft fire embers settling, distant floorboard creak)

VELVETEEN RABBIT
What is REAL? Does it mean having things that buzz inside you and a stick-out handle?

SKIN HORSE
Real isn't how you are made. It's a thing that happens to you.

The Skin Horse turns his worn head gently toward the Rabbit.

SKIN HORSE
When a child loves you for a long, long time, not just to play with, but REALLY loves you, then you become Real.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Does it hurt?

SKIN HORSE
Sometimes. When you are Real you don't mind being hurt.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Does it happen all at once, like being wound up, or bit by bit?

SKIN HORSE
It doesn't happen all at once. You become. It takes a long time.

(SOUND: night wind soft at the window)

The Skin Horse's bald patches catch the moonlight. The Rabbit listens, still.

SKIN HORSE
Generally, by the time you are Real, most of your hair has been loved off, and your eyes drop out and you get loose in the joints and very shabby.

SKIN HORSE
But these things don't matter at all, because once you are Real you can't be ugly, except to people who don't understand.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I suppose you are real?

The Skin Horse only smiles — wise, gentle, certain.

SKIN HORSE
The Boy's Uncle made me Real. That was a great many years ago. Once you are Real you can't become unreal again. It lasts for always.

The Rabbit sighs. He looks at his own neat whiskers and perfect pink ears, longing and a little afraid.

(MUSIC: soft wistful nursery theme, low strings)

INT. NURSERY - NIGHT

NANA — middle-aged, apron, practical hair pinned up — bustles at bedtime. THE BOY sits up in bed, searching.

(SOUND: cupboard doors, hurried footsteps)

NANA
Here, take your old Bunny! He'll do to sleep with you!

She drags the Velveteen Rabbit out by one ear and puts him into the Boy's arms. The Boy clasps him close.

(SOUND: bedclothes rustling, a contented child sigh)

INT. BOY'S BED - NIGHT

The Boy hugs the Rabbit very tight. Sometimes he rolls over on him. Sometimes he pushes him far under the pillow. A night-light burns on the mantelpiece.

(SOUND: steady breathing, soft night-light hiss)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
That night, and for many nights after, the Velveteen Rabbit slept in the Boy's bed.

The Boy whispers, making tunnels under the bedclothes.

THE BOY
(whispering)
Burrows. Like the real rabbits live in.

When the Boy sleeps, the Rabbit snuggles under his warm chin. The Boy's hands stay clasped round him all night long.

(MUSIC: gentle lullaby motif)

INT. BOY'S BED - NIGHT (LATER)

Time has passed. The Rabbit's velveteen fur is shabbier. His tail is coming unsewn. Pink is rubbed from his nose where the Boy has kissed him. He does not notice. He is happy.

(SOUND: quiet breathing, distant owl)

EXT. GARDEN - DAY

Spring. THE BOY wheels the Rabbit in a barrow, then builds fairy huts under raspberry canes. They picnic on the grass. Butterflies drift past.

(SOUND: birdsong, wheelbarrow squeak, leaves in light wind)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Spring came, and they had long days in the garden, for wherever the Boy went the Rabbit went too.

Dusk. The Rabbit lies alone on the lawn, wet with dew, earthy from flower-bed burrows. NANA arrives with a candle, grumbling, and rubs him with her apron.

(SOUND: evening insects, Nana's footsteps on gravel)

INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

NANA carries the damp Rabbit in. THE BOY sits up in bed, arms out.

(SOUND: bedsprings, soft candle flicker)

NANA
You must have your old Bunny! Fancy all that fuss for a toy!

THE BOY
Give me my Bunny! You mustn't say that. He isn't a toy. He's REAL!

The Velveteen Rabbit goes still in wonder. Boot-button eyes, polish long gone, take on a look of wisdom and beauty.

(MUSIC: warm resolving chord, soft and sure)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
When the little Rabbit heard that he was happy, for he knew that what the Skin Horse had said was true at last.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The nursery magic had happened to him, and he was a toy no longer. He was Real. The Boy himself had said it.

INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - MORNING

NANA picks the Rabbit up. She pauses, studying his face.

(SOUND: morning birds outside the open window)

NANA
I declare if that old Bunny hasn't got quite a knowing expression!

EXT. WOOD - EVENING

Long June light. Bracken and tall trees. THE BOY settles the Velveteen Rabbit in a little nest among the ferns, then wanders off among the trees.

(SOUND: leaves rustling, ants in dry grass, distant boyish calls)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
One evening, while the Rabbit was lying there alone, he saw two strange beings creep out of the tall bracken near him.

Two WILD RABBITS — furry, brand-new, seams invisible — creep close, noses twitching. They change shape as they move: long and thin, then fat and bunchy. The Velveteen Rabbit stares, hunting for clockwork.

(SOUND: soft padded feet on earth)

WILD RABBIT
Why don't you get up and play with us?

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I don't feel like it.

WILD RABBIT
Ho! It's as easy as anything.

The Wild Rabbit hops sideways and stands on his hind legs.

WILD RABBIT
I don't believe you can!

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I can! I can jump higher than anything!

WILD RABBIT
Can you hop on your hind legs?

The Velveteen Rabbit freezes. The back of him is all one piece, like a pincushion. He has no hind legs at all.

(SOUND: bracken whispering, a wood thrush far off)

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I don't want to!

The Wild Rabbit stretches his neck and looks hard.

WILD RABBIT
He hasn't got any hind legs! Fancy a rabbit without any hind legs!

SECOND WILD RABBIT laughs. The first whirls and dances till the Velveteen Rabbit grows dizzy.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I have! I have got hind legs! I am sitting on them!

WILD RABBIT
Then stretch them out and show me, like this!

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I don't like dancing. I'd rather sit still!

The Wild Rabbit comes so close his long whiskers brush the Velveteen Rabbit's ear — then wrinkles his nose, flattens his ears, and jumps backward.

(SOUND: sudden scramble of feet in leaves)

WILD RABBIT
He doesn't smell right! He isn't a rabbit at all! He isn't real!

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I am Real! I am Real! The Boy said so!

Footsteps. THE BOY runs past. With a stamp and a flash of white tails the wild rabbits vanish into the bracken.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Come back and play with me! Oh, do come back! I know I am Real!

Only ants run to and fro. The bracken sways where the strangers passed. The Velveteen Rabbit is all alone. Little white moths flutter out as the sun sinks. The Boy returns and carries him home.

(MUSIC: lonely descending phrase)

(SOUND: moth wings, evening stillness)

INT. NURSERY - DAY

Weeks later. The Rabbit is very old and shabby — whiskers loved off, ear linings grey, brown spots faded, shape nearly lost. THE BOY still holds him as if he were beautiful.

(SOUND: rain on the window, quiet room)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He didn't mind how he looked to other people, because the nursery magic had made him Real, and when you are Real shabbiness doesn't matter.

INT. NURSERY - NIGHT

THE BOY is ill. His face is flushed. He talks in his sleep. His little body burns so hot it heats the Rabbit. Strange people come and go. A light burns all night. The Velveteen Rabbit lies hidden under the bedclothes and never stirs.

(SOUND: labored breathing, low adult murmurs, clock striking)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was afraid that if they found him some one might take him away, and he knew that the Boy needed him.

The Rabbit creeps close to the pillow and whispers plans of gardens and raspberry thickets into the Boy's ear. Slowly the fever turns. The Boy sits up with picture-books. The Rabbit cuddles at his side.

(MUSIC: fragile hope, thin and careful)

INT. NURSERY - DAY

Bright sun. Windows wide open. THE BOY has been carried out to the balcony in a shawl. The Velveteen Rabbit lies tangled in the bedclothes, only his head peeping out. NANA and the DOCTOR — dark coat, serious bag — stand near the bed.

(SOUND: light curtain stir, distant garden birds)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The Boy was going to the seaside to-morrow. The room was to be disinfected, and all the books and toys that the Boy had played with in bed must be burnt.

NANA
How about his old Bunny?

DOCTOR
That? Why, it's a mass of scarlet fever germs! Burn it at once.

NANA hesitates. The Doctor is firm.

DOCTOR
Nonsense! Get him a new one. He mustn't have that any more!

The Rabbit's boot-button eyes stare. He does not understand — not yet.

(SOUND: a sack shaken open, books thudding in)

EXT. GARDEN BY FOWL-HOUSE - EVENING

Behind the fowl-house, at the end of the garden, a sack sits among rubbish and old picture-books. The gardener is busy elsewhere. The sack has been left untied. The Velveteen Rabbit wriggles until his shabby head comes through the opening.

(SOUND: hens settling, cool evening wind in raspberry canes)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was shivering a little, for he had always been used to sleeping in a proper bed, and by this time his coat had worn so thin it was no longer any protection.

He looks toward the raspberry thicket where he once played. Memories pass — fairy huts, ants on his paws, the day he knew he was Real, the wise Skin Horse.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Of what use was it to be loved and lose one's beauty and become Real if it all ended like this?

A real tear trickles down his little shabby velvet nose and falls to the ground.

(SOUND: one soft drop on earth — then stillness)

(MUSIC: hush, then a high delicate shimmer)

Where the tear fell, a mysterious flower pushes from the soil — slender emerald leaves, a blossom like a golden cup. The Rabbit forgets to cry and only watches. The blossom opens. Out steps the NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY — dress of pearl and dew-drops, flowers at her neck and in her hair, face like the most perfect flower.

(SOUND: petals unfurling, a faint chime of air)

She gathers him up and kisses his damp velveteen nose.

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY
Little Rabbit, don't you know who I am?

VELVETEEN RABBIT
(looking up)
...

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY
I am the nursery magic Fairy. I take care of all the playthings that the children have loved.

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY
When they are old and worn out and the children don't need them any more, then I come and take them away with me and turn them into Real.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Wasn't I Real before?

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY
You were Real to the Boy, because he loved you. Now you shall be Real to every one.

(MUSIC: rising wonder, light and clear)

EXT. WOOD - NIGHT

Moon risen. Bracken fronds shine like frosted silver. The Fairy flies with the Rabbit into the open glade. Wild rabbits dance with their shadows on the velvet grass — then stop and stand in a ring, staring.

(SOUND: soft wing-rush of flight, then hush of the glade)

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY
I've brought you a new playfellow. You must be very kind to him and teach him all he needs to know in Rabbit-land.

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY
For he is going to live with you for ever and ever!

She kisses the little Rabbit again and sets him on the grass.

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY
Run and play, little Rabbit!

He sits quite still, shy, remembering his missing hind legs — not knowing the last kiss changed him altogether. Something tickles his nose. Without thinking he lifts a hind toe to scratch it.

(SOUND: a tiny scratch, then grass under new feet)

He stares. He has hind legs. Brown fur, soft and shiny, instead of dingy velveteen. Ears that twitch by themselves. Whiskers so long they brush the grass.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He gave one leap and the joy of using those hind legs was so great that he went springing about the turf on them.

He jumps sideways, whirls as the others do, wild with joy. When at last he stops to look for the Fairy, she has gone.

(SOUND: many soft rabbit feet on turf, happy thumps)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was a Real Rabbit at last, at home with the other rabbits.

(MUSIC: full bright resolution, then gentle fade toward spring)

EXT. WOOD - DAY

Spring again. Warm sun. THE BOY — a little taller, same fair hair — plays among the trees. Two rabbits creep from the bracken and peep at him.

(SOUND: leaves underfoot, mild breeze, distant birds)

One rabbit is brown all over. The other has strange markings under his fur, as though long ago he had been spotted, and the spots still show through. About his soft nose and round black eyes there is something familiar.

THE BOY
(softly, to himself)
Why, he looks just like my old Bunny that was lost when I had scarlet fever!

The spotted rabbit watches the child who first helped him to be Real. Then both rabbits slip back into the bracken.

(SOUND: bracken parting, then only wind and birds)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
But he never knew that it really was his own Bunny, come back to look at the child who had first helped him to be Real.

Sunlight holds the empty glade a moment longer. A single bracken frond sways.

(MUSIC: quiet storybook close, warm and still)

FADE OUT.

THE END
